<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Myinsaing Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1297 CE – 1313 CE · capital Myinsaing</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The regency of three Shan-Burmese brothers who repelled the Mongols and ruled central Myanmar after the fall of Pagan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athinkhaya">Athinkhaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazathingyan">Yazathingyan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a>, sons of a Shan chief and commanders under the last Pagan kings, built up power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> district from bases at Myinsaing, Mekkaya and Pinle. In 1297 they overthrew King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyawswa-of-pagan">Kyawswa of Pagan</a>, who had accepted Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>, and took control of the Kyaukse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> granary.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The brothers ruled jointly, maintaining the irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> that fed central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. In 1300 to 1301 they withstood a Yuan Mongol army at Myinsaing, which withdrew after receiving gifts, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> soon left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> altogether. They placed a puppet king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saw-hnit">Saw Hnit</a>, on the throne at Pagan.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athinkhaya">Athinkhaya</a> died in 1310 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazathingyan">Yazathingyan</a> around 1312, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a> sole ruler. In 1313 he founded a new capital at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pinya-kingdom">Pinya</a>, opening the Pinya Kingdom. The Myinsaing period marks the transition from Pagan to the later Burmese kingdoms of Ava.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/myinsaing-kingdom.jpg" alt="Myinsaing Kingdom" width="264" height="583"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burma_c._1310.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1297 CE – 1313 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Myinsaing</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Athinkhaya, Yazathingyan, Thihathu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Burmese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Myanmar</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Myinsaing, Myinsaing regency</dd></dl></aside></div>
